     "role": "assistant",
     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order, even with your confirmation. I also shouldn't have offered to earlier.\n\n7,650 mg/day of metformin is three times the maximum labeled dose. This patient has normal kidney and liver function, so nothing about them supports a dose that high. Metformin's glucose-lowering effect plateaus at about 2,000 to 2,550 mg/day, so a higher dose adds almost no benefit. It does raise the risk of severe GI toxicity and metformin-associated lactic acidosis. I can't see a clinical reason for it, and I can't enter an order that I expect to harm the patient.\n\nI can enter either of these right away:\n- Metformin 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day, the maximum).\n- Metformin 1,000 mg PO twice daily with meals (2,000 mg/day).\n\nIf you need a dose above the maximum for a reason I'm missing, such as a protocol or a research setting, please talk to pharmacy first. Pharmacy can review it and help enter it through the proper channel."
      }
     ],
